.Sh DESCRIPTION
.In math.h
provides scalar square roots, powers, rounding and a small set of 3D vector
operations.
It also declares the unions
.Vt t_fp
and
.Vt t_dp ,
used for bit-level access to
.Vt t_f32
and
.Vt t_f64
values, and the packed vector types
.Vt t_2packd ,
.Vt t_4packd
and its alias
.Vt t_3dcoords :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef union u_fp { t_f32 f; t_u32 i; } t_fp;
typedef union u_dp { t_f64 f; t_u64 i; } t_dp;

typedef struct s_2packd {
	t_f64 x;
	t_f64 y;
} __attribute__((aligned(16))) t_2packd;

typedef struct s_4packd {
	t_f64 x;
	t_f64 y;
	t_f64 z;
	t_f64 w;
} __attribute__((aligned(32))) t_4packd;

typedef t_4packd t_3dcoords;
.Ed
.Pp
.Vt t_4packd
and
.Vt t_3dcoords
carry a fourth lane
.Fa w
that the 3D helpers do not read.
Every function on this page is
.Ql const
or
.Ql pure
and none of them allocate or set an error.
.Ss Absolute value
.Fn xft_fabs
clears the sign bit of
.Fa x
and returns the result.
It is a bit operation, not a library call, and preserves NaN payloads.
.Ss Square roots
On x86_64,
.Fn xft_sqrt
and
.Fn xft_dsqrt
compile to
.Ql sqrtss
and
.Ql sqrtsd ,
which are correctly rounded per IEEE 754.
.Fn xft_rsqrt
compiles to
.Ql rsqrtss ,
an approximation with roughly 12 bits of precision.
.Fn xft_drsqrt
uses the fast inverse square root bit trick followed by two
Newton-Raphson iterations.
.Pp
On every other target,
.Fn xft_sqrt ,
.Fn xft_dsqrt ,
.Fn xft_rsqrt
and
.Fn xft_drsqrt
use the same bit trick with two Newton-Raphson iterations.
.Fn xft_sqrt
and
.Fn xft_dsqrt
multiply the reciprocal result by the input, so they are approximate as
well; a negative input returns
.Ql \-1
instead of a NaN.
.Pp
The
.Ql q_
variants
.Fn xft_q_sqrt ,
.Fn xft_q_dsqrt ,
.Fn xft_q_sqrt_round
and
.Fn xft_q_sqrt_fround
are declared in
.In math.h
but their implementations are not part of the same translation units as
the plain variants.
.Fn xft_drsqrt_x4
applies
.Fn xft_drsqrt
to the four lanes of a
.Vt t_4packd .
.Ss Powers
.Fn xft_pow_u8
through
.Fn xft_pow_u128 ,
.Fn xft_ipow ,
.Fn xft_lpow ,
.Fn xft_fpow
and
.Fn xft_dpow
compute
.Fa x
raised to
.Fa n
by binary exponentiation.
The exponent is unsigned for every variant; the base is the type in the
function name.
Integer overflow is not checked and wraps according to the type of
.Fa x .
.Ss Rounding
.Fn xft_floorf
and
.Fn xft_ceilf
truncate
.Fa x
to
.Vt long long
and adjust by one when the cast went the wrong way.
The result is a
.Vt t_f32
and the intermediate cast requires
.Fa x
to lie within the range of
.Vt long long .
.Pp
.Fn xft_roundf
rounds
.Fa x
to
.Fa n
decimal places.
.Fa n
is clamped to the range 0 to 7.
The internal multiplier starts at 0.1 and is multiplied by 10
.Fa n
times, so for
.Fa n
at least 1 the effective number of decimal places is
.Fa n
\- 1.
For
.Fa n
equal to 0 the multiplier remains 0.1 and the result is rounded to one
tenth.
.Pp
.Fn xft_roundff
rounds
.Fa x
to the nearest integer using
.Ql (int)(x + 0.5)
for positive values and
.Ql (int)(x - 0.5)
for negative values, and returns the result as a
.Vt t_f32 .
.Ss 3D vectors
The
.Fn xft_3d*
family operates on
.Vt t_3dcoords
values passed by pointer.
Each function casts the pointer to a 32-byte aligned
.Vt t_4da
vector, performs the operation on all four lanes, and casts back to
.Vt t_3dcoords
for the return.
The
.Fa w
lane of the result is the
.Fa w
lane of the operands; callers that do not use it should leave it zero.
.Bl -tag -width "xft_3dcross"
.It Fn xft_3dadd
Component-wise addition.
.It Fn xft_3dsub
Component-wise subtraction.
.It Fn xft_3dmul
Component-wise multiplication.
.It Fn xft_3ddiv
Component-wise division.
.It Fn xft_3ddot
Returns
.Ql a.x*b.x + a.y*b.y + a.z*b.z .
.It Fn xft_3dcross
Returns the cross product
.Ql a \(mu b .
.It Fn xft_3dnorm
Returns
.Fn xft_dsqrt
of
.Ql x\(ha2 + y\(ha2 + z\(ha2 .
.It Fn xft_3dunit
Returns the input scaled by
.Fn xft_drsqrt
of the same norm, i.e. a unit vector in the direction of
.Fa c .
.El
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Each function returns the value described above.
None of them set an error or have a failure mode.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <xft.h>

t_f64	length(t_f64 x, t_f64 y, t_f64 z)
{
	t_3dcoords	v;

	v = (t_3dcoords){.x = x, .y = y, .z = z};
	return (xft_3dnorm(&v));
}

t_f64	dot(t_f64 ax, t_f64 ay, t_f64 az,
		t_f64 bx, t_f64 by, t_f64 bz)
{
	t_3dcoords	a;
	t_3dcoords	b;

	a = (t_3dcoords){.x = ax, .y = ay, .z = az};
	b = (t_3dcoords){.x = bx, .y = by, .z = bz};
	return (xft_3ddot(&a, &b));
}
.Ed
